e place in  August 2015  we built six functional software  products and a couple of them including  the sort of machine learning based  pharmacogenomics prediction pipeline are  still in active development in fact the  Agathon's in total we've developed about  80 software products and about 40 of  them are stable or still in active  development this is a picture this is a  subset of the people that were in that  particular hackathon and this is  approximately what at NCBI hackathon  looks like although as I'll discuss  later now they're in many many different  locations all of the tools that we build  in NCBI hackathons are totally  open-source and we put everything on  github as I'll talk about later we also  containerize things so we we also see  manifestations on docker hub Jupiter  other places as well we build several  things in NCBI hackathons and things end  up in several places some things end up  being worked into NCBI production I'd  say that's sort of a minority of things  other things end up being part of our  standalone bioinformatics tools but  other things really end up being part of  educational experiences and we work them  in two webinars but also in two larger  scale online genomics courses and one  thing I'm particularly proud of  we built an educational resource for RNA  seek which is self-guided and you can go  to right now and fire up some cloud  instances either on Google Cloud engine  or Amazon Web Services and teach  yourself how to do RNA seek mapping with  star high set and magic blasts like I  said we worked this into an online  workshop the first five lectures of that  online workshop are available on YouTube  right now 2016 we really sort of  extended the frequency these hackathons  became very  popular and we started having them in  other places including Cold Spring  Harbor is a B and also hack seek was an  NCBI style hagath on that was run in  Vancouver and I was a participant not an  organizer of that event and it was a  very special very large NCBI style  hackathon so then in January we went  back to our main product or sort of the  regular hackathon rotation and we really  started building things that integrated  with larger software pipelines including  things like SRA 2r which you can see  here in the middle of your screen which  is an R package and we'll probably end  up in bioconductor  as a way to port data from SRA into our  frameworks we also started partnering  with other groups and I mentioned hack  seek before we were able to also start  building novel things like for example  ways to compare structural variants to  structural variant databases and that's  that's really been a neat focus of some  of our work is working with structural  variants as there are in my opinion not  enough tools to analyze that kind of  data in the bioinformatics community  like I said we started working with bio  Khanda a little bit with linux Peru as  well as bioconductor a lot of the things  in our hackathons deal well with the  sequence read archive like the SRA to  our package I mentioned earlier we're  also interested in fostering community  involvement with these hackathons it'll  talk about that even more at the end but  we were able to build a platform for  metadata categorization by NLM indexers  so they were able to go ahead and  annotate metadata in geo which has been  a bio informatics community hobby for  the last 15 or 20 years and we were they  actually annotated all of the metadata  for 14,000 drosophila RNA seek data sets  which we then used in a collaboration  with flybase we've also been able to  harmonize some of the metadata in in DB  gap with the nih come  repository another thing that I'm  particularly excited about is the  ability to do really experimental things  so to our knowledge until this point was  very very difficult for people to put  full genomes into a graph and few people  had attempted it and so in a hackathon  we ran in Cold Spring Harbor we were  able to load seven