ting scientific  publication in my personal opinion is is  very important one of the things that's  very accessible that we built in a  hackathon is fan var and you can see the  URL here it's fan mark colorado edu this  will generate word clouds of concepts  koh mentioned with snips in PubMed  abstracts but my friends liked to remind  me that word clouds really aren't  serious science but what is serious  science is this will also generate d3  rendered networks of snips and  particular p.m. IDs so that may be  useful to some of the folks in the  audience and to the question before this  is an example of some of the sort of  biomedical text mining stuff we do and  integrating with genomics something  that's sort of pure text mining we built  something called pub runner I'm very  excited about pub runner basically what  it is is a framework for natural  language processing tools that  continuously updates the PubMed corpus  and this this framework could also be  used to update other corpora for natural  language processing research and we're  working on packaging this and  collaborating with a lot of groups to to  allow them to auto update their PubMed  corpus if you're interested in this  please send me an email it's been B and  dot B USB Y at nih gov the head  developer of this in a hackathon jay  cleaver is currently working in my group  for the next four weeks on this project  so this would be a particularly good  time to contact me we've also been able  to take other people's software products  and really put them in more reproducible  frameworks so we have a common workflow  language pipeline for epigenomics called  screw and if you do epigenomics I'd  really encourage you to check that out  because I think that reproducibility  is really important but particularly in  the epigenomic space and we've been able  to collaborate in this particular case  with ga4gh so this is actually a slide  generated by the Global Alliance data  working group and it shows NCBI is 