inars on API but I'll show you  that these are Jupiter notebooks that  we're building to enhance NCBI education  and we really hope to build a whole  suite of computational resources that  are used to leverage ncbi databases and  we think that's going to be really  helpful to the community and if you're  interested in getting involved in those  projects whether it's through hackathons  or through some other means please feel  free to contact myself or Peter Guber  next I want to show you some really  cutting-edge stuff that we built just a  couple of weeks ago and these are think  projects that I'm very excited about in  them I'm really proud to say that  they're still fairly stable and in  ongoing development one of the things we  built is a very simple easy to use tool  for looking at antimicrobial resistance  genes in metagenomics datasets um this  really heavily leverages magic blast  which is a new flavor of blast optimized  to deal with next generation sequencing  reads that we built at ncbi and also use  the card database to identify some of  these meta genomic resistance genes  although I think it's very attractive to  integrate databases as well as putting  in some learning modules so I really  encourage you to go to github grab this  code check it out it's docker honest  it's very easy to leverage and use on  anybody's Linux system and right now the  cancer genomics cloud is free and you  can also leverage it there so that may  be something to check out we're also  really interested in streaming data and  and what this particular thing does so  we we built a database of adapters that  are present in the SRA the really neat  thing about that is if you know what the  adapters are in SRA for your particular  data set then you can trim the adapters  on the fly so if you want to  if you want to do some assembly and  generate context now you actually don't  have to do fast queue dump anymore  you can simply trim your adapters on the  fly and then go ahead and build contacts  from streaming data which is something  that I think is very attractive the NCBI  infrastructure but with just also with  genomic data getting larger and if you  have any general questions on that I'd  love to answer them on FTP or once again  you can email me directly I was  particularly proud of a team they came  from a particular academic institution  and they really wanted to build a viewer  for an excellent Mouse aging data set  and what they actually ended up doing  was building really modular types of  viewers for existing RNA seek viewers  and I think that's really important  because there are currently 35 RNA seek  viewers that are up in the community and  supported about thirty-five and I think  it's really important to say well if you  have a particular thing that you want to  be able to view it's it's great to build  modular tools that you can insert into  those viewers and that's something we've  become really really interested in and  at the end of the hackathon we were able  to generate movies looking at Mouse  aging with and without infection to flu  and what's really amazing to me is even  at this extremely zoomed out level of  the mouse genome you're able to see  responses to influenza over long periods  of time there is a question about  virtual hackathons but I will wait till  the end to get into that and this was a  really experimental project and and I  think I look forward to iterate on this  project I'm really to be frank this team  was filled with brilliant people but I'm  not sure that random flipping is the  most prudent obfuscation model but but  basically what this does is it sends a  robot into anybody's data sets looks for  particular variants and that if it's  desired by the people that own the data  it off you skates the data so it's no  longer personally identifiable the nice  thing is that switch can be flipped back  off to generate a full data  matrix if some collaboration agreement  is reached base and which is obviously  consistent with the contagion consents  on the data we've also been helping out  with other people's hackathons that's  something I'm particularly proud of  we've helped out with some things at by  OIT as well as recently an artificial  intelligence genomics hackathon in San  Francisco and these are things we look  forward really look forward to to  working with also on the NCBI hackathons  github site which I'll put up in the  last slide you can find repo for being  able to run your own NCBI style  hackathons which I'd be happy to help  out with as well but if you just want to  roll your own please please feel free to  take any pieces of this and do that I  think we'd like to see more of this and  there's there's certainly no shortage of  folks that are interested in also our  next hackathon is in Pittsburgh  Pennsylvania at the end of September and  one of the things we're likely going to  be working on is it out of the box a  sort of automatic hackathon builder and  that's that's something I'm pretty  excited about so I mentioned that there  are publications out of the hackathons  in one of the places we published these  hackathon results as well as allow other  people to publish these hackathon  results is in f1000 research and and  they've been really sort of wonderful in  in working with us on publishing  hackathon tools but certainly some  communication of these tools has gone to  other journals as well you can also  follow at DC genomics on Twitter so and  in other places on Twitter we try to  advertise things in these hackathons  this is a picture of some of the folks  in the New York gene of the most recent  New York genome Center hackathon in June  and that was really a fantastic  experience a lot of folks from both that  metro area as well as folks that came in  from across the country and even other  countries so like I said here's a list  of some of the upcoming hackathons if  any of you are in California there will  almost certainly be a northern Cal  for Nia hackathon the first week in  April likely either at UCSC or UCSF and  we're trying to work on a Southern  California hackathon that may occur in  January so please check this site for  that finally the actual repos can be  found both at this this github  organization and then sort of stable and  other other projects that are continuing  to be developed can be found at this URL  NCBI hackathon C github do so thank you  very much for your time via blog post  coming out on NCBI insights about some  of the products from the last three  hackathons Boulder Colorado New York  genome Center as well as the one we just  ranted in CBI and now I'm going to take  questions and I actually have one queued  up here and and it's an excellent  question so the question is are there  plans to host virtual hackathons so  we've done quite a few experiments with  virtual hackathons and one thing we've  seen is that we would we involve virtual  people we actually see not even sort of  less production but dramatically less  production from the people that are  involved online at least that's been our  experience so far however the last  experiment we did on that we had a  hackathon veteran somebody who had been  to several hackathons working part time  to sort of work on modular tools and  that was a very positive experience  we've also had people do quite a bit of  work online after the hackathons so so  what I could see a model of is sort of  hackathon veterans or people coming to  in-person hackathons of this style and  then continuing to work independently  that said of course there are other  hackathon models that work very well  online sort of long term competitive  hackathons tend to work very well online  and one of the things we've talked about  doing is in the virus discovery space  as well as the antimicrobial resistance  space is hosting sort of long-term  online hackathons to really leverage  some of the tools we've built in the  in-person hackathons and use them to  analyze data sets I would also like to  mention that there will be a second year  of hack seek so that's the big awesome  genomics hackathon in Vancouver I  mentioned and you can actually go there  and register at hack sitcom so a neat  thing is that there are now other  hackathons running on this model someone  else asked does the hackathon have a  slack channel or team and in fact this  is a great question so each hackathon  has its own slack organization which  obviously persists after the hackathons  we originally ran them through Google  Groups  and then we moved to slack and that's  been very popular we also have a  LinkedIn group for a hackathon alumni  where jobs are posted people can talk  about issues that sort of thing and we  also make announcements and that's  something I'm really proud of because  what I think we're doing with the NCBI  hackathons and other NCBI style  hackathons like hack see is we're  creating a community of really both  excellent computational biologists but I  think we're also seeing evidence  suggests that suggests that these people  work well with other people  and and really in my experience are  really kind of nice people and so I  think this actually also makes an  excellent tool for building a phenomenal  computational biology work force that  alumni group may actually expand from  being mostly a LinkedIn presence to  slack and other avenues of social media  I'd also like to mention that in the  third week of June 2018 we're having a  hackathon in Boulder Colorado as part of  a genomics conference and we'd really  like that to be mostly what we're  calling a champions hackathon so mostly  hackathon alumni  that come to Boulder and really work to  integrate some of the projects and build  sort of larger scale bioinformatics  platforms so like I said bringing those  people back and and having them continue  to work for the bioinformatics community  finally we're starting to integrate with  other bioinformatics hackathons that you  may be familiar with so I've been to the  bosk Agathon's and we're talking with  them about integrating as well as sort  of the general bio hackathons community  and elixir those are things I look  forward to telling you about sometime  soon great well thank you for the really  great questions people that ask  questions I think with that and actually  typically many times I don't put the Q&amp;A  session on the recording but I think  perhaps for this one will be popular so  thanks to everybody who attended and all  you hack a thon alumni out there there's  a couple in attendance here and with  that I think we'll go ahead and sign off  if you have any further questions please  feel free to email me and we just have  we have one or two spots available for  the Pittsburgh hackathon the deadline is  closed but if you're very very  interested and you're able to go to  Pittsburgh September 25th 26th and 27th  please go ahead and sign up thank you  very much everybody and have a fantastic  rest of the day have a fantastic week  and tune in next week for an NCBI  webinar on NCBI api's I think you'll  find that there's a lot more NCBI api's  than you thought there were  you
 

